WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:05.120
Spiders don’t just use their silk threads to build webs.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.800
They also spin sails to catch the breeze,

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:11.180
sometimes flying hundreds of kilometers.

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:13.640
This behavior — called ballooning —

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:17.640
has helped spiders disperse all over the globe.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.300
To find out more about how spiders launch,

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.180
scientists in Germany collected some large,

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:27.580
orb-web-spinning crab spiders in a park outside Berlin.

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:30.080
They put the spiders on a mushroom-shaped

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:32.960
platform outdoors, and observed how the spiders

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:36.860
behaved under different wind conditions.

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:39.400
They also put the arachnids in a wind tunnel,

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.660
where the scientists could carefully control

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:44.160
wind speed and temperature.

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:47.350
The spiders didn’t release their silk balloons at random.

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:51.020
Instead, they tested the wind by raising their front legs

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:53.720
in the air for about 6 seconds.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.900
When it was cold and windy, the spiders hid.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:59.380
But when the breeze was warm and gentle

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.380
— less than 3 meters per second —

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.200
the spiders spun out a

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.400
triangular sail made of silk threads.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.840
Spiraling updrafts lifted the sails,

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.700
and the spiders were on their way.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.760
It’s clear these crab spiders wait for the right conditions

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:18.000
before takeoff. The next question is,

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.200
can they control where they land?

